Feature: tour parse
  As an author of a tour
  I want to check that my .tour file is valid
  So that I can fix mistakes before sharing or viewing it

  @ft:1
  Scenario: Parsing a well-formed tour file
    Given a .tour file with three valid slides
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  it prints the number of slides found
    And   it lists each slide's file:line with a narration preview
    And   it exits successfully

  @ft:2
  Scenario: A slide missing its location line
    Given a .tour file where a slide has no "@ file:line" line
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  it reports a structural error naming the line in the tour file
    And   it exits with a non-zero status

  @ft:3
  Scenario: Multiple structural errors are all reported at once
    Given a .tour file with two malformed slides
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  it reports an error for each malformed slide, not just the first
    And   those errors are ordered by the slide number

  @ft:4
  Scenario: A slide pointing at a file that doesn't exist
    Given a .tour file with a slide referencing a file that does not exist
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  it reports a location error naming that slide
    And   it exits with a non-zero status

  @ft:5
  Scenario: A slide pointing past the end of its file
    Given a .tour file with a slide whose line number is beyond the end of the referenced file
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  it reports a location error naming that slide
    And   it exits with a non-zero status

  @ft:6
  Scenario: A location with no line number defaults to the first line
    Given a .tour file with a slide written as "@ path/to/file.rb" with no line number
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  the slide is listed pointing at line 1

  @ft:7
  Scenario: File paths resolve relative to the tour file, not the working directory
    Given a .tour file that references another file with a relative path
    When  I run `tour parse` on it from a different working directory
    Then  the reference resolves relative to the tour file's own directory

  @ft:8
  Scenario: The listed location matches what was written in the tour file
    Given a .tour file with a slide written as "@ ../lib/app/auth.ex:42"
    When  I run `tour parse` on it from a different working directory
    Then  the listed location for that slide is "../lib/app/auth.ex:42"
    And   it is not shown as an absolute path

  @ft:9
  Scenario: Slide locations are column-aligned
    Given a valid .tour file whose slides have file:line locations of different lengths
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  every slide's narration preview starts in the same column

  @ft:10
  Scenario: A successful parse is shown in green on a terminal
    Given a valid .tour file
    When  I run `tour parse` on it in a terminal
    Then  the slide count line is printed in bold green

  @ft:11
  Scenario: Slide locations are colored distinctly from their narration
    Given a valid .tour file
    When  I run `tour parse` on it in a terminal
    Then  each slide's file:line is printed in a color distinct from its narration preview

  @ft:12
  Scenario: No color codes appear when output is not a terminal
    Given a valid .tour file
    When  I run `tour parse` on it with output piped, not to a terminal
    Then  the output contains no ANSI escape codes

  @ft:35
  Scenario: A location with a scroll offset is shown as written
    Given a .tour file with a slide written as "@ lib/app/auth.ex:42:4"
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  the listed location for that slide is "lib/app/auth.ex:42:4"

  @ft:36
  Scenario: An empty line number with a scroll offset defaults to line 1
    Given a .tour file with a slide written as "@ lib/app/auth.ex::4"
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  the slide is listed pointing at line 1

  @ft:37
  Scenario: An empty scroll offset is the same as no scroll offset
    Given a .tour file with a slide written as "@ lib/app/auth.ex:42:"
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  the slide is listed pointing at line 42

  @ft:38
  Scenario: Non-digit content in a location field is malformed
    Given a .tour file with a slide written as "@ lib/app/auth.ex:42:abc"
    When  I run `tour parse` on it
    Then  it reports a structural error naming the line in the tour file
    And   it exits with a non-zero status

